The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT) && (in_nmi() || in_hardirq()))
    return NULL;
Reason:
On PREEMPT_RT, local_lock becomes a sleeping lock. The original check only blocks NMI and hard IRQ contexts but does not handle other situations where preemption is disabled (e.g., tracepoints with elevated preempt_count). Consequently, when preemption is disabled, kmalloc_nolock() proceeds to call local_lock_irqsave() which tries to acquire a sleeping lock, causing a "sleeping function called from invalid context" BUG.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT) && !preemptible())
    return NULL;
Reason:
The condition `!preemptible()` returns true whenever the kernel is in a non-preemptible context, which includes NMI, hard IRQ, and any context where preemption is disabled (preempt_count > 0). This directly expresses the constraint that a sleeping lock (local_lock on RT) cannot be taken when preemption is disabled, thereby correctly catching all problematic scenarios.

